          {
            ":db/id": 17592186060688,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186060942,
              ":tutorial/content": "The row echelon form of a matrix is *not* unique. In the previous example, after the first row operation we arrived at \n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}{\\color{red}1} & 3 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}-10} & 5\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nThis augmented matrix is already in row echelon form! However, it is always useful to do an extra step and obtain $1$'s along the main diagonal, like we did previously:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}{\\color{red}1} & 3 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}1} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nHaving $1$'s along the main diagonal makes the final back substitution process a little easier.",
              ":tutorial/id": "ee4b134d-2908-5930-86bb-32537e0d8347",
